#+TITLE: An Awk Conundrum
#+AUTHOR: Brian Kamotho
#+DATE: 2019-10-30

It started with an elementary task. Given a csv file like this one:

#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
first_name,last_name,phone_number
William,Robert,2547123456
Mary,Jane,2547123456
John,Michael,2547123456
#+END_EXAMPLE

Quote the phone numbers in double quotes. This should do it:
#+BEGIN_SRC awk
BEGIN {FS=","; OFS=","}
NR > 1 {printf("%s,%s,\"%s\"\n", $1, $2, $3)}
#+END_SRC

This is what I got instead:

#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
"illiam,Robert,"2547123456
"ary,Jane,"2547123456
"ohn,Michael,"2547123456
#+END_EXAMPLE

Strange. I thought I had missed something about how Awk's printf works, so I
scoured the docs again. Maybe something about double quotes. No luck. I thought
something might be wrong with Gnu Awk, so I tried Busybox awk and Nawk. They
were all consistent, so it couldn't be a bug.

I tried ~"%s,%s,*%s*\n"~ instead, thinking that it could be the double
quotes. Same thing, only with asterisks.

Next, I tried ~"%s,%s,*%s\n"~. This one worked as expected:
#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
William,Robert,*2547123456
Mary,Jane,*2547123456
John,Michael,*2547123456
#+END_EXAMPLE

I thought it must be the character after the format specifier, so I tried
~"%s,%s,%s*\n"~:
#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
*illiam,Robert,2547123456
*ary,Jane,2547123456
*ohn,Michael,2547123456
#+END_EXAMPLE

It can't be. To test the hypothesis, I tried ~"%s,%s*\n"~, and it worked as
expected:
#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
William,Robert
Mary,Jane
John,Michael
#+END_EXAMPLE

Alright, so it's something to do with the phone number? I was getting impatient,
and this was supposed to be easy and someone needed those phone numbers quoted.
So I spun off some ad-hoc python to do it, planning to figure out what was going
on with Awk later. It was also kinda embarrassing. Anyway, the python that saved
the day:
#+BEGIN_SRC python
import csv
import os

with open(
    "query_result.csv", mode="r", newline=""
) as inputfile, open("out.csv", mode="w+", newline="") as outputfile:
    reader = csv.reader(
        inputfile,
        quoting=csv.QUOTE_MINIMAL,
        doublequote=True,
        lineterminator="\n",
        strict=True,
    )
    for row in reader:
        row[2] = '"%s"' % (row[2])
        outputfile.write("%s,%s,%s\n" % (row[0], row[1], row[2]))
#+END_SRC

Later, I came back to this problem, and I still couldn't figure it out so I
emailed a friend. He tried it on his own sample input and couldn't reproduce
what I was seeing. I sent him my sample input and he quickly and colorfully
validated my sanity.

We started hypothesizing. He discovered that formatting the phone number as an
integer (~"%s,%s,*%d*\n"~) produced the expected result:
#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
William,Robert,*2547123456*
Mary,Jane,*2547123456*
John,Michael,*2547123456*
#+END_EXAMPLE
That only deepened the mystery. Why couldn't he reproduce this with his own
sample input, which actually had two integer columns?

In my early attempts to sanitize the output, I had tried copying the buffer into
another file in emacs and noticed I couldn't reproduce the behavior with the new
file. I suspected emacs was changing something. Meanwhile, my friend had figured
it out: it was carriage returns. Curious about what could be different between
his sample file and mine, he had opened my file in a hex editor.

Sure enough, ~cat -v~ showed the carriage returns in the original file:
#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
first_name,last_name,phone_number^M
William,Robert,2547123456^M
Mary,Jane,2547123456^M
John,Michael,2547123456^M
#+END_EXAMPLE

Piping the output of awk through ~cat -v~ also showed what awk was printing:
#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
William,Robert,*2547123456^M*
Mary,Jane,*2547123456^M*
John,Michael,*2547123456^M*
#+END_EXAMPLE

So this had nothing to do with Awk at all! This explains why formatting the
phone numbers as integers doesn't produce garbled output: the ~\r~ isn't
printed. It also explains why the file I created in emacs didn't produce garbled
output: the default ~buffer-file-coding-system~ is ~undecided-unix~. But why
didn't the python script above produce garbled output as well?

With ~newline=""~ and ~lineterminator="\n"~, it ought to be parsing the file
just like awk is. I'm also printing the phone number as a string, so what gives?

#+BEGIN_SRC python
>>> import os
>>> import csv
>>> inputfile = open("sample.csv", mode="r", newline="")
>>> reader = csv.reader(
>>>     inputfile,
>>>     quoting=csv.QUOTE_MINIMAL,
>>>     doublequote=True,
>>>     lineterminator="\n",
>>>     strict=True,
>>> )
>>> rows = []
>>> for row in reader:
>>>     rows.append("%s,%s,*%s*\n" % (row[0], row[1], row[2]))
>>> rows
>>> [
>>>     "first_name,last_name,*phone_number*\n",
>>>     "William,Robert,*2547123456*\n",
>>>     "Mary,Jane,*2547123456*\n",
>>>     "John,Michael,*2547123456*\n",
>>> ]
>>> 
#+END_SRC

What happened to the carriage return? The answer is at the bottom of the docs
for the csv module:
#+BEGIN_QUOTE
...It should always be safe to specify newline='', since the csv module does its
own (universal) newline handling.
#+END_QUOTE

The glossary describes universal newline handling as:
#+BEGIN_QUOTE
A manner of interpreting text streams in which all of the following are
recognized as ending a line: the Unix end-of-line convention '\n', the Windows
convention '\r\n', and the old Macintosh convention '\r'. See PEP 278 and PEP
3116, as well as bytes.splitlines() for an additional use.
#+END_QUOTE

That's...neat. Can we make Awk do universal newline handling?
We can, in Gawk at least:
#+BEGIN_QUOTE
The ability for RS to be a regular expression is a gawk extension. In most other
awk implementations, or if gawk is in compatibility mode (see section
Command-Line Options), just the first character of RS’s value is used.
#+END_QUOTE
Hence:
#+BEGIN_SRC awk
BEGIN {FS=","; OFS=","; RS="\r\n|\r|\n"}
NR > 1 {printf("%s,%s,*%s*\n", $1, $2, $3)}
#+END_SRC
#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
William,Robert,*2547123456*
Mary,Jane,*2547123456*
John,Michael,*2547123456*
#+END_EXAMPLE

Could it be that simple? I was curious if the order mattered, so I switched the
regex to ~\r|\n|\r\n~. Same result as above. I thought the carriage return would
be matched first. That's what ~man perlre~ says, at least:

#+BEGIN_QUOTE
Alternatives are tried from left to right, so the first alternative found for
which the entire expression matches, is the one that is chosen. This means that
alternatives are not necessarily greedy. For example: when matching "foo|foot"
against "barefoot", only the "foo" part will match, as that is the first
alternative tried, and it successfully matches the target string.
#+END_QUOTE

I switched the regex to ~"\r|\n"~:
#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
,,**
William,Robert,*2547123456*
,,**
Mary,Jane,*2547123456*
,,**
John,Michael,*2547123456*
,,**
#+END_EXAMPLE

Interesting. Switched to ~"\n|\r"~. Same thing.  Awk's regex is not
perl-compatible.
#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
$ awk '{match($0, /foo|foot/); printf("%s\n", substr($0, RSTART, RLENGTH))}' <<<football
foot
$ awk '{match($0, /foot|foo/); printf("%s\n", substr($0, RSTART, RLENGTH))}' <<<football
foot
$ grep -Po 'foo|foot' <<<football
foo
$ grep -Eo 'foo|foot' <<<football
foot
#+END_EXAMPLE

The documentation explains it:
#+BEGIN_EXAMPLE
The alternation applies to the largest possible regexps on either side. 
#+END_EXAMPLE

It also matches the leftmost longest string. So with ~\r|\n|\r\n~, it would
match ~\r\n~ first, so we'd parse files encoded in the Windows convention
properly.  Therefore this /is/ a working universal newline handling
implementation.

-----

In retrospect, it should have been obvious it was carriage returns, and there
were clues all over the place. But I've used Gnu/Linux exclusively for so long I
forgot there were alternative end-of-line conventions.

